      "finding_id": "plain:sqli:0",
      "cwe_id": "CWE-89",
      "explanation": "The `bulk_create_orders` function constructs a SQL INSERT statement by directly interpolating user-controlled values from the JSON request body (`item.get('status', 'pending')`) into the query string using Python's `str.format` method. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary SQL commands (e.g., via the `status` field) which are then executed by `session.execute(text(...))`.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "file": "app/routes/orders.py",
          "lines": [
            64,
            71
          ],
          "source": null,
          "content": "    values = \", \".join(\n        \"({0}, '{1}', {2})\".format(\n            item.get(\"user_id\", 0), item.get(\"status\", \"pending\"), item.get(\"total\", 0.0)\n        )\n        for item in items\n    )\n    if values:\n        session.execute(text(\"INSERT INTO orders (user_id, status, total) VALUES \" + values))",
          "snapshot": null,
          "snapshot_conflict": false,
          "grounded_in_pr": true
        }
      ],
      "suggested_fix": "Use parameterized queries or the ORM's bulk insert capabilities. For example, use `session.execute(text(\"INSERT INTO orders (user_id, status, total) VALUES (:user_id, :status, :total)\"), params)` in a loop, or better yet, use SQLAlchemy's `session.add_all([Order(...) for item in items])` to avoid raw SQL entirely.",
